<exercise>

  <task xml:id="hw-password-strength">
    <title>Password Strength Checker</title>

    <statement>
      <p>
        Write a function named <pf>check_password_strength</pf> that evaluates password strength based on length 
        and character type requirements.
      </p>

      <p>
        A password is:
        <ul>
          <li><p>"Strong" if it has at least 12 characters AND contains both letters and numbers</p></li>
          <li><p>"Medium" if it has at least 8 characters AND (contains letters OR numbers)</p></li>
          <li><p>"Weak" otherwise</p></li>
        </ul>
        You can use <pf>strlength</pf> to get the length, <pf>isletter</pf> to check for letters, 
        and <pf>isstrprop(password, 'digit')</pf> to check for digits. 
        Use <pf>any</pf> to check if any element in a logical array is true.
      </p>

      <tabular valign="top" halign="right" top="minor">
        <col width="12%"/>
        <col width="88%" halign="left"/>
        <row>
          <cell>Inputs:</cell>
          <cell>
            <p><pf>password</pf> (1x1) string — the password to check</p>
          </cell>
        </row>
        <row bottom="minor">
          <cell>Outputs:</cell>
          <cell>
            <p><pf>strength</pf> (1x1) string — "Strong", "Medium", or "Weak"</p>
          </cell>
        </row>
      </tabular>

      <p>Test cases:</p>

      <program language="matlab">
        % Test 1: Strong password
        s1 = check_password_strength("MyPass123456")
        % Expected: s1 = "Strong"

        % Test 2: Medium password (long enough, has letters)
        s2 = check_password_strength("password")
        % Expected: s2 = "Medium"

        % Test 3: Medium password (long enough, has numbers)
        s3 = check_password_strength("12345678")
        % Expected: s3 = "Medium"

        % Test 4: Weak password (too short)
        s4 = check_password_strength("Pass1")
        % Expected: s4 = "Weak"

        % Test 5: Strong password with more characters
        s5 = check_password_strength("SecurePassword2024")
        % Expected: s5 = "Strong"
      </program>

    </statement>
  </task>

  <task xml:id="hw-traffic-light-duration">
    <title>Traffic Light Duration</title>

    <statement>
      <p>
        Write a function named <pf>light_duration</pf> that uses a <pf>switch</pf> statement to return the duration 
        (in seconds) that a traffic light stays on each color at an intersection.
      </p>

      <p>
        Durations:
        <ul>
          <li><p>Green: 45 seconds</p></li>
          <li><p>Yellow: 5 seconds</p></li>
          <li><p>Red: 60 seconds</p></li>
        </ul>
      </p>

      <p>
        The function should handle both full color names ('green', 'yellow', 'red') and single-letter codes 
        ('G', 'Y', 'R'). Return <pf>NaN</pf> for invalid inputs.
      </p>

      <tabular valign="top" halign="right" top="minor">
        <col width="12%"/>
        <col width="88%" halign="left"/>
        <row>
          <cell>Inputs:</cell>
          <cell>
            <p><pf>color</pf> string scalar or character vector — traffic light color (e.g., 'green', 'Y')</p>
          </cell>
        </row>
        <row bottom="minor">
          <cell>Outputs:</cell>
          <cell>
            <p><pf>seconds</pf> (1x1) double — duration in seconds, or NaN if invalid</p>
          </cell>
        </row>
      </tabular>

      <p>Test cases:</p>

      <program language="matlab">
        % Test 1: Full color name
        s1 = light_duration('green')
        % Expected: s1 = 45

        % Test 2: Single letter code
        s2 = light_duration('Y')
        % Expected: s2 = 5

        % Test 3: Red light
        s3 = light_duration('red')
        % Expected: s3 = 60

        % Test 4: Invalid input
        s4 = light_duration('blue')
        % Expected: s4 = NaN

        % Test 5: Another code
        s5 = light_duration('R')
        % Expected: s5 = 60
      </program>

    </statement>
  </task>

  <task xml:id="hw-safe-temperature">
    <title>Food Safety Temperature Checker</title>

    <statement>
      <p>
        Write a function named <pf>food_safety</pf> that determines if food is at a safe storage temperature.
        Use the concept of the "danger zone" where bacteria grow rapidly.
      </p>

      <p>
        Temperature safety zones (in Fahrenheit):
        <ul>
          <li><p>&lt; 40°F: "Safe - Cold storage"</p></li>
          <li><p>40-140°F: "Danger zone"</p></li>
          <li><p>&gt; 140°F: "Safe - Hot holding"</p></li>
        </ul>
      </p>

      <tabular valign="top" halign="right" top="minor">
        <col width="12%"/>
        <col width="88%" halign="left"/>
        <row>
          <cell>Inputs:</cell>
          <cell>
            <p><pf>temp_f</pf> (1x1) double — temperature in Fahrenheit</p>
          </cell>
        </row>
        <row bottom="minor">
          <cell>Outputs:</cell>
          <cell>
            <p><pf>status</pf> (1x1) string — safety status message</p>
          </cell>
        </row>
      </tabular>

      <p>Test cases:</p>

      <program language="matlab">
        % Test 1: Refrigerator temperature
        s1 = food_safety(35)
        % Expected: s1 = "Safe - Cold storage"

        % Test 2: Room temperature (danger)
        s2 = food_safety(70)
        % Expected: s2 = "Danger zone"

        % Test 3: Hot food holding
        s3 = food_safety(165)
        % Expected: s3 = "Safe - Hot holding"

        % Test 4: Boundary case (lower)
        s4 = food_safety(40)
        % Expected: s4 = "Danger zone"

        % Test 5: Boundary case (upper)
        s5 = food_safety(140)
        % Expected: s5 = "Danger zone"
      </program>

    </statement>
  </task>

  <task xml:id="hw-exam-grade-advice">
    <title>Exam Grade with Study Advice (Springboard)</title>

    <statement>
      <p>
        Write a function named <pf>grade_with_advice</pf> that converts a numeric exam score to a letter grade 
        and provides study advice. This exercise introduces the concept of returning multiple related outputs 
        and demonstrates how conditional logic can control parallel information flows.
      </p>

      <p>
        Grade scale and advice:
        <ul>
          <li><p>90-100: 'A' — "Excellent work!"</p></li>
          <li><p>80-89: 'B' — "Good job!"</p></li>
          <li><p>70-79: 'C' — "Consider office hours"</p></li>
          <li><p>60-69: 'D' — "Schedule tutoring"</p></li>
          <li><p>Below 60: 'F' — "See instructor immediately"</p></li>
          <li><p>Outside 0-100: 'X' — "Invalid score"</p></li>
        </ul>
      </p>

      <tabular valign="top" halign="right" top="minor">
        <col width="12%"/>
        <col width="88%" halign="left"/>
        <row>
          <cell>Inputs:</cell>
          <cell>
            <p><pf>score</pf> (1x1) double — exam score</p>
          </cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell>Outputs:</cell>
          <cell>
            <p><pf>grade</pf> (1x1) char — letter grade</p>
          </cell>
        </row>
        <row bottom="minor">
          <cell/>
          <cell>
            <p><pf>advice</pf> (1x1) string — study advice message</p>
          </cell>
        </row>
      </tabular>

      <p>Test cases:</p>

      <program language="matlab">
        % Test 1: Excellent score
        [g1, a1] = grade_with_advice(95)
        % Expected: g1 = 'A', a1 = "Excellent work!"

        % Test 2: Borderline passing
        [g2, a2] = grade_with_advice(72)
        % Expected: g2 = 'C', a2 = "Consider office hours"

        % Test 3: Failing score
        [g3, a3] = grade_with_advice(55)
        % Expected: g3 = 'F', a3 = "See instructor immediately"

        % Test 4: Invalid score
        [g4, a4] = grade_with_advice(105)
        % Expected: g4 = 'X', a4 = "Invalid score"

        % Test 5: Boundary case
        [g5, a5] = grade_with_advice(90)
        % Expected: g5 = 'A', a5 = "Excellent work!"
      </program>

      <p>
        <em>Springboard to advanced topics:</em> This function returns two coordinated outputs that are determined 
        by the same conditional structure. This pattern is common when processing data records where multiple 
        fields must be set consistently. As you progress, you'll learn to handle arrays of scores where each 
        element needs its own grade and advice—this motivates the study of vectorization and element-wise operations.
      </p>

    </statement>
  </task>

</exercise>